      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\left(\\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}\\right) = 12\\), we start by letting \\( y = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} \\). The equation then becomes:\n\n\\[ y^2 + y = 12. \\]\n\nRearranging the terms, we get a standard quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ y^2 + y - 12 = 0. \\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic equation as:\n\n\\[ (y - 3)(y + 4) = 0. \\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the possible solutions for \\( y \\):\n\n\\[ y = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = -4. \\]\n\nNext, we substitute back \\( y = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} \\) to find the corresponding values of \\( x \\).\n\n1. For \\( y = 3 \\):\n\n\\[ 3 = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}, \\]\n\nwe cross-multiply to get:\n\n\\[ 3(3x - 4) = 4x - 16, \\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[ 9x - 12 = 4x - 16. \\]\n\nRearranging terms gives:\n\n\\[ 9x - 4x = -16 + 12, \\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[ 5x = -4. \\]\n\nThus, we get:\n\n\\[ x = -\\frac{4}{5}. \\]\n\n2. For \\( y = -4 \\):\n\n\\[ -4 = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}, \\]\n\nwe cross-multiply to get:\n\n\\[ -4(3x - 4) = 4x - 16, \\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[ -12x + 16 = 4x - 16. \\]\n\nRearranging terms gives:\n\n\\[ -12x - 4x = -16 - 16, \\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[ -16x = -32. \\]\n\nThus, we get:\n\n\\[ x = 2. \\]\n\nThe greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is therefore:\n\n\\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
