      "text": "**Source:** New York State Integrative Math Competition 2020 - Spring\n\nTo solve the given problem, we start by introducing a substitution to simplify the equation. Let \\( y = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} \\). The given equation can be rewritten in terms of \\( y \\):\n\n\\[ y^2 + y = 12. \\]\n\nThis is a standard quadratic equation. To solve for \\( y \\), we first move the constant term to the other side of the equation:\n\n\\[ y^2 + y - 12 = 0. \\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ (y - 3)(y + 4) = 0. \\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions for \\( y \\):\n\n\\[ y - 3 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y + 4 = 0, \\]\n\\[ y = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = -4. \\]\n\nRecall that \\( y = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} \\). We now have two equations to solve for \\( x \\):\n\n1. \\( \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} = 3 \\)\n2. \\( \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} = -4 \\)\n\n### Solving the first equation:\n\n\\[ \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} = 3. \\]\n\nWe clear the fraction by multiplying both sides by \\( 3x-4 \\):\n\n\\[ 4x - 16 = 3(3x - 4). \\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\n\\[ 4x - 16 = 9x - 12, \\]\n\\[ -16 + 12 = 9x - 4x, \\]\n\\[ -4 = 5x, \\]\n\\[ x = -\\frac{4}{5}. \\]\n\n### Solving the second equation:\n\n\\[ \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4} = -4. \\]\n\nWe clear the fraction by multiplying both sides by \\( 3x-4 \\):\n\n\\[ 4x - 16 = -4(3x - 4). \\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\n\\[ 4x - 16 = -12x + 16, \\]\n\\[ 4x + 12x = 16 + 16, \\]\n\\[ 16x = 32, \\]\n\\[ x = 2. \\]\n\nAnalyzing the results from both solutions:\n\nThe first solution, \\( x = -\\frac{4}{5} \\), is valid because it does not make the denominators zero (which would occur if \\( x = -\\frac{4}{5} \\) in the original equation form, but does not happen here).\n\nThe second solution, \\( x = 2 \\), is also valid because it does not make the denominators zero (neither in the original nor simplified form).\n\nTo determine which value (if either) is the greatest possible value of \\( x \\), we simply compare the values:\n\n\\[ -\\frac{4}{5} < 2. \\]\n\nThus, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
